I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: “Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own. The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” The two reply texts are, first, {"a":1} [{"b":2}], and second, {"c":3} [{"b":2}]. In the first, the only top-level object is {"a":1}; the object {"b":2} is nested inside an array and therefore not a candidate, so the rule requires the check to return {"a":1}. In the second, the only top-level object is {"c":3}; again {"b":2} is nested inside an array and not a candidate, so the rule requires the check to return {"c":3}. The check, however, scans for every “{” and raw-decodes from it without checking whether that brace is inside an array, so in both texts it will append the nested {"b":2} as a top-level candidate and, because that nested object appears last in the text, it will return {"b":2} for both. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returned {"a":1} for the first text and {"c":3} for the second, or otherwise distinguished them as the rule requires.